! SEED=20
! GAME=../entharion/zcode-infocom/cutthroats-r23-s840809.z3

stand up

# This is important in the game. If your watch runs down, you
# can't keep track of the time.

wind watch
take note
read note
open dresser
take room key
open door
out

# The book stays behind, so the door must actually LOCK -- and
# there is a trap here: after walking out, the door stands open,
# and a bare "lock door" earns a polite "You should close it
# first" that a replay marches straight past. (The statuette
# lesson again: silent refusals.) With the door truly locked the
# Weasel never lays a hand on the book -- he strolls right past
# you on Wharf Road at 8:19 instead of burgling your room -- and,
# crucially, he keeps his nine o'clock at The Shanty. Probed the
# other timelines: leave the door open, or let the lock silently
# fail, and he prowls the inn, drifts to the Back Alley, and Pete
# announces "Weasel ain't gonna show." No Weasel, no deal, dead
# game -- decided at this door, an hour before anyone tells you.

close door
lock door

d. out. e. e. e. e. s
sit down
order breakfast
wait
order water
drink water
eat breakfast

# The Weasel slinks in while breakfast settles -- "At Pete's
# request, the Weasel joins you" -- and Johnny whispers about the
# sunken treasure: the deal is on. If this scene ever plays as
# "Weasel ain't gonna show" instead, the door lock upstream
# silently failed; see above.

yes
stand up
exit
w. w. w. w. sw. sw. nw

# Johnny Red arrives with this seed.

wait. wait. wait. wait

# The Weasel arrives with this seed.

wait. wait

# Pete the Rat arrives with this seed.

wait. wait

# You  can only recover treasures from two of the ships,
# the Sao Vera and the S.S. Leviathan. The other wrecks
# are only red herrings, and you don't have to bother
# with them. Which of the two real wrecks you will dive
# for depends on the item you are shown by Johnny Red.
# If he shows you the gold coin, it's the Sao Vera; if
# it's the dinner plate, then the ship is the Leviathan.
# This seed shows the dinner plate.

wait

# Johnny gives instructions. You have to get the minutes
# of longitude and latitude of the wreck, $500, and then
# be at Point Lookout at 10:45.

wait
wait

se. ne. ne. s. u. s
unlock door
open door
s
take passbook
take all from closet

# Since we're diving for the Leviathan, you need to get the
# scuba gear from the closet. (The scuba gear not needed for
# the Sao Vera).

n
close door
lock door
d. n. e. e. e. e. se. sw

# You don't want McGinty to see you lugging your gear around.

drop suit, tank, mask, and flippers
se. s. sw. n
withdraw $500
s. ne. se

# The walkthroughs say "return to Ocean Road, where you go
# southeast to Point Lookout" -- and that last climb is the whole
# ballgame. The south end of Ocean Road is NOT Point Lookout; the
# lookout is up the small cliff path to its southeast. Stop one
# room short and everything downstream unravels on schedule:
# McGinty strolls by, sees the passbook lying in the open road,
# and pockets it with a long look at you -- while Johnny climbs
# to the empty lookout at 10:36, waits twenty minutes, and gives
# up. Up HERE the passbook is out of sight (McGinty prowls the
# road below the whole time, probed and confirmed), and Johnny
# finds you at his appointed hour.

drop passbook

wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait

# "Well? Did you bring the money?" Show him, and Johnny levels
# with you: Hevlin gave him the dinner plate, and the boat hangs
# on one question -- "Is the treasure more than 200 feet deep?"
# The answer is NOT in the game. Reading the book of shipwrecks
# yields only "This book is included in your Cutthroats package":
# the wreck table lived in the printed booklet in the box, 1984
# copy protection, which is why every walkthrough simply knows
# it. The Leviathan lies shallow -- hence our scuba gear -- so
# the answer is no.

show money to johnny
no
nw. n. n. nw. w. s

# Wait until McGinty leaves.

wait
wait
wait

# One more beat: the salesman spends this turn busy with JOHNNY,
# who hands over his list, pays his share, and sets the whole
# clock in one speech -- "We'll leave on the Night Wind at high
# tide... Deliver the stuff half an hour before high tide...
# Better be on board then to watch the stuff." Pay before that
# plays out and the $69 goes nowhere; then every purchase below
# bounces off "I can't sell you anything until you pay the $69
# of Johnny Red's," nothing is ever delivered, and hours later
# Johnny scrubs the expedition on the dock with "Outfitters
# never delivered the stuff." Five polite refusals, one dead
# game.

wait

pay $69
buy flashlight
buy shark repellent.

# If you're diving for the Sao Vera, that's all you need.
# However, if you are diving for the Leviathan, you need
# more things.

buy C battery
buy putty
buy electromagnet
rent small air compresser

n. e. se. s. s. sw. w. w

# Soon McGinty will appear, and a short while later, Weasel.
# The two men will go off to a corner and talk. Then Weasel
# will hand something to McGinty, and board the Ferry.

wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait

# Three more beats finish the show: "The Weasel hands something
# to McGinty and then boards the ferry," the ferry pulls away,
# and McGinty strolls off east -- which is the real gift, because
# he walks the Ocean Road ahead of us and turns north just as we
# duck southwest into the alley. The gear gets collected with his
# back turned, which is exactly how "don't let McGinty see you
# lugging your gear around" prefers it.

wait
wait
wait

e. e. ne. n. nw
take all
w. w

# Standing behind the vacant lot, which is next door to McGinty's.
# Wait here, and McGinty will come by, heading from East to West.
# Continue waiting, and he will soon re-appear, going from West to
# East (he is walking along Wharf Road, of course).

wait
wait
wait
wait

w
open window
enter window
take envelope
read envelope
s. e. n. n. n

# Enter whichever ship has been rented for the dive, which for this
# seed is the Night Wind.

w. n. d

# You don't want Weasel to know you have it (he'll kill you), and if
# you show it to Johnny now, you'll cancel the expedition.

hide envelope under bed

# "Better be on board to watch the stuff": twelve minutes in the
# bunks covers it. The delivery boy trundles his cart through the
# boat, the Weasel slips below, and Johnny takes up station in
# the Captain's Cabin -- aft of here, past the galley (Pete's
# stew, and water) and the lounge.

wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait
wait

# Aft to the cabin with the numbers. The coordinates are copy
# protection like the depth was: they come from the book of
# shipwrecks in the game package, 25 and 25 for the Leviathan.
# The syntax that works is talking to Johnny directly.

s. s. s
johnny, the latitude is 25
johnny, the longitude is 25

# Back to the bunks. "We'll be leaving shortly. Get some sleep."
# The Night Wind sails at high tide, the bunk claims you on the
# next turn, and Johnny shakes you awake at the dive site. Mind
# the stomach on arrival: the hunger and thirst daemons are
# shouting by then, and Pete's stew and the galley water are one
# room aft. Eat and drink before suiting up.

n. n. n
wait
wait

stand up
put on wet suit
put on flippers
take mask
take tank

fore

take battery
take drill
open drill
put battery in drill
close drill

get all except compressor
fill tank with compressor

aft
get envelope
aft
eat stew
drink water
aft. aft

show johnny the envelope
n. u
put on tank
put on mask

# Time for the dive!

dive
open shark repellent
turn on flashlight
d. d. d. d. aft
read sign
open door
aft
touch magnet to mine
turn on magnet
drop the magnet
u
remove tank
aft. aft
turn on the drill
drill the lock

# You must do the next thing right away!

turn off drill

# The walkthroughs' urgency is real, but its reason is hidden:
# the case took water THE INSTANT the safe popped -- it rode in
# on that rush of escaping air, before any hand touched it -- and
# no sealing order beats it there (probed: even a pre-squeezed
# glob applied the very next turn finds "some water" already
# inside). Worse, "empty case" is booby-trapped -- "In the
# process of emptying the case, you manage to get the stamps
# wet" -- and any water left aboard sloshes on the climb out and
# ruins them anyway. So: a race, then a repair. Run the case to
# the air pocket before the rising water tops the shelf -- six
# turns, exactly -- donning the tank mid-sprint in the dining
# room, because the floor hole refuses a hand-carried tank and
# the narrow passages refuse a worn one. And never, ever DROP
# the tank: it takes the airhose with it, and you with the hose.

take case
fore. fore
put on tank
d
fore
u

# Sanctuary: a passenger compartment still full of air, kept dry
# by its closed aft door. Now the repair: drill a drain hole in
# the case -- "The water pours out of the hole" -- with what
# proves to be the drill's dying breath, the battery sputtering
# out on this very act. Then one glob of putty seals crack and
# drain hole together. The stamps never feel a drop.

turn on drill
drill case
turn off drill
open tube
squeeze tube
put glob on crack

# Out and up: through the storage locker, up past the sleeping
# quarters, and two hundred feet of blue back to the Night Wind.
# "Good job, matey!" Two hundred fifty points of a possible two
# hundred fifty, the rank of a rich diver, and a thought for
# Hevlin.

d
fore
u
u
u. u. u. u
